{"translation": "For when the organs are in their natural state, the quantity of the pneuma is always proportional to the dilatations [794] of them; but not, indeed, when they are also disposed contrary to nature: rather, it is possible at times for the thorax to be distended to the greatest extent, and yet for only a little of the outside air to be inhaled. For there are many conditions, if we remember anything of what was said before, by which the lung, being constricted, is unable to receive a sufficient quantity of air. Inspirations, therefore, do not come about in proportion to the states of all the respiratory organs, but only of the rough arteries. But it is not possible to discern the dilatation of these by any one of the senses. Therefore one ought not even to direct one's attention to the respiratory organs whenever we wish to discern the quantity of the inspiration. For it suffices to examine the air alone: how much is inhaled, and how much, in turn, is exhaled. For neither do we infer anything about the amount of foods from the bulk of the belly; here too it suffices to examine only the quantity of what is eaten.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 51, "surface": "πνεύμων", "english": "lung", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 62, "surface": "ἀναπνευστικῶν", "english": "respiratory", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 65, "surface": "διαθέσεσιν,", "english": "states", "uncertain": "y"}, {"target_no": 69, "surface": "τραχειῶν", "english": "rough", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 70, "surface": "ἀρτηριῶν", "english": "arteries", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 72, "surface": "εἰσπνοαὶ", "english": "inspirations", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 79, "surface": "αἰσθήσεων", "english": "senses", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 131, "surface": "ποσὸν", "english": "quantity", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["διαθέσεσιν (in 'ταῖς διαθέσεσιν': the sense seems to require something like διαστάσεσιν/διαστολαῖς, 'distensions'; rendered 'states')", "ὄγκοις (bulk/swellings of the belly)"]}
