{"translation": "For delirium is not altogether able to obliterate completely the alteration arising from the pain, unless it should itself be [850] great and exceedingly violent, and the pain small and faint; the pain, however, when it is strong, completely overcomes the difficulty of breathing arising from the delirium, so that it does not show itself at all. In the same way, if delirium is combined with any of the other conditions, it will produce now one form of breathing and now another, according as it prevails, or is prevailed over, or is of equal strength, or at one time, as it may happen, prevails, and again at another moment of the disease is prevailed over. We shall show, then, that in the books of the Epidemics too he teaches this very thing through the patients written up in them. It will be shown clearly and plainly once we have first said a little about the purpose of Hippocrates. For the ancients suppose that he writes down everything that befalls the sick, just as Thucydides does; but this is not so; rather, this very point is that in which the writings of Hippocrates are most opposed to those of Thucydides.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 14, "surface": "ἀλλοίωσιν,", "english": "alteration", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 31, "surface": "ἀμυδρός·", "english": "faint", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 44, "surface": "δύσπνοιαν,", "english": "difficulty of breathing", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 57, "surface": "διαθέσεων", "english": "conditions", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 64, "surface": "ἀναπνοῆς", "english": "breathing", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 107, "surface": "ἀῤῥώστων.", "english": "patients", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 119, "surface": "προαιρέσεως", "english": "purpose", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 146, "surface": "ἐναντιώτατον", "english": "most opposed", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": []}
