{"translation": "Neither, then, can these patients escape the notice [854] even of any chance physician, nor can their conditions, upon which difficulty of breathing follows of necessity. For the conditions are clear, and the form of the difficulty of breathing is evident, and it occurs in many people very nearly every day. But it has been shown many times that Hippocrates writes nothing of things so clear. For Thucydides wrote down what befell the sick as a layman for laymen, but Hippocrates as a practitioner of the art for practitioners of the art. Let us now, then, speak of the very patients who suffered difficulty of breathing, in whose case he did not see fit to state, I do not say the form of the difficulty of breathing, but not even simply this alone, that they had difficulty in breathing.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 18, "surface": "δύσπνοια.", "english": "difficulty of breathing", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 27, "surface": "δυσπνοίας", "english": "difficulty of breathing", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 56, "surface": "ἰδιώτης", "english": "layman", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 61, "surface": "τεχνίτης", "english": "practitioner of the art", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["οὐχ ὅπως"]}
