{"translation": "But here Hippocrates himself explained clearly how, when the sputum is held back, the breathing becomes frequent by way of the intermediate smallness; in the case of the pain, however, he left it out, since, I think, he himself has said in many places that the functions [918] of the organs are hindered by pain, and since we too are able, even if we had not heard it from him beforehand, to discover it from what plainly appears. Besides, then, teaching us clearly both these things in this passage, namely that pain and narrowness of space alike, by making the breathing small, at once show it to be frequent as well, he further teaches this too: that these are not the only causes of frequent breathing. First, in saying (and it is said thus in the case of the pain), he makes it plain that the breathing is already frequent because of the other cause, when he says that it becomes more frequent because of the pain, since, I think, here too we are able to reason out that the febrile heat in any case makes it frequent. Second, in saying that the sputum, being made viscid by the frequent and copious breathing, as has already been said, prevents its upward passage from taking place, being unconcocted. For of the frequent breathing the pain is the cause, but of the copious breathing it is not this but the febrile heat. And here too he quite clearly said not 'large breathing' but 'copious'.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 11, "surface": "πτυέλῳ", "english": "sputum", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 20, "surface": "μικρότητος·", "english": "smallness", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 61, "surface": "ἄμφω", "english": "both", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 94, "surface": "αἴτια.", "english": "causes", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 133, "surface": "πυρεκτικὴν", "english": "febrile", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 134, "surface": "θερμασίαν", "english": "heat", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 156, "surface": "πτύελον", "english": "sputum", "uncertain": "n"}, 